abstract: >-
  We develop a source-resolved spectral criterion for long-range tensor exchange
  coupled to conserved stress-tensor sources. Exact channel elimination carries
  the propagation kernel, physical source, readout and direct response together,
  so that the observable residue is independent of the chosen intermediate
  variables. For an isolated critical subspace, common-space operator estimates
  control the residue at the displaced root of a finite approximation. A positive
  Källén–Lehmann representation then identifies the residue with the weight of a
  zero-invariant-mass atom, including the case in which the atom lies at the
  endpoint of continuous support. We prove separately that the null-shell part of
  a complete positive, Lorentz-covariant conserved stress-tensor measure is
  longitudinal and therefore has no transverse-traceless atomic weight. In a
  compact spectral realisation, the internal tensor vertices are carried into an
  external two-helicity response. For localised stationary matter, the conserved
  energy vertex gives a mixed residue proportional to the two total energies, and
  spatial resolvent estimates determine the leading potential and force together
  with their finite-size corrections. After the collective tensor is normalised
  through its matter vertex, a single dimensional calibration fixes the overall
  Planck scale, while the spectral residue fixes the corresponding dimensionless
  exchange strength. Together, these results specify the positivity, helicity,
  universality and nonlinear consistency conditions under which the collective
  tensor admits an Einstein–matter effective description.
keywords:
  - "Emergent gravity"
  - "Stress-tensor correlation functions"
  - "Källén–Lehmann representation"
  - "Massless spectral atoms"
  - "Long-range tensor exchange"
  - "Schur–Feshbach reduction"
  - "Analytic Fredholm theory"
  - "Matter coupling"
